# udev rules for GlideFS ublk block devices.
#
# Install:
#   sudo cp deploy/udev/99-glidefs-ublk.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/99-glidefs-ublk.rules
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
#
# These run during the KOBJ_ADD uevent in the kernel's `add_disk` path —
# BEFORE userspace can open /dev/ublkbN and before any bios are routed
# through the device. That timing is critical: writing
# `/sys/block/.../queue/scheduler` triggers `blk_mq_freeze_queue_wait`,
# which on kernel 6.17+ waits for armed FETCH_REQ uring_cmds. If we
# tried to apply these settings AFTER the daemon armed FETCH_REQs (e.g.
# from a `tokio::task::spawn_blocking` post-`add_disk`), the freeze
# would block forever. Applying them at add-time avoids that entirely.
#
# Tunables we set:
#
# - scheduler=none — userspace handles I/O ordering; `mq-deadline`'s
#   per-CPU staging adds latency for no benefit on ublk (no seeks).
#
# - wbt_lat_usec=0 — disable Write-Back Throttle. Default (~2ms target)
#   misreads our userspace+S3 round-trip latency as queue congestion
#   and clamps dispatch to single-digit IOPS.
#
# - add_random=0 — don't feed ublk completion events into the kernel
#   entropy pool. Useful for HDDs (real entropy from seek timing);
#   pointless for ublk. Saves a tiny bit of CPU per request.
#
# - read_ahead_kb=0 — glidefs does its own readahead inside the
#   handler (`trigger_readahead` in `block/handler.rs`), tuned for our
#   pack/chunk geometry. Kernel readahead duplicates work and pollutes
#   the hot-set cache with prefetches we'd issue ourselves anyway.

ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="ublkb*", \
    ATTR{queue/scheduler}="none", \
    ATTR{queue/wbt_lat_usec}="0", \
    ATTR{queue/add_random}="0", \
    ATTR{queue/read_ahead_kb}="0"
